preferred-citation:
  type: generic
  title: "From Monovalent Dev to Bivalent DevOps to Trivalent DevIntOps: Intelligence as the Third Continuous Loop in Software Engineering"
  authors:
    - family-names: Chertorogov
      given-names: Pavel
  year: 2026
  month: 10
  version: "draft"
  url: "https://devintops.org/v1/"
  abstract: >-
    DevIntOps puts what a software system learns under a contract: what each
    lesson records, what it may change, how its effect is checked and how it
    leaves. Many agentic SDLC proposals put an AI agent into every box of the DevOps
    figure-eight. That can make each box faster, but it doesn't by itself carry
    experience from one task to the next. This paper makes learning an
    explicit, persistent and measured third loop next to Development and
    Operations: Intelligence (Understand, Predict, Orchestrate, Learn, with
    memory and judgment at its core), which turns outcomes such as escaped
    bugs, incidents, review feedback, wasted work and changes that went well
    into lessons that change how each stage decides. The same logical pattern
    applies to the engineering system and to the decisions a product makes for
    its users. It is a step toward self-improving software systems, with a
    bound: the loop aims to make later decisions better, while people keep the
    goals, the permissions and the scoring.
